The State and society attach importance to developing the labour force;
raising labour discipline, promoting vocational skills and the occupations of the
people; and safeguarding the rights and legitimate interests of workers.

(amended). The National Assembly has the following rights and duties:

    1. To consider and adopt the Constitution and the laws;

    2. To consider and adopt amendments to the Constitution, the socio-economic
development plan and the State budget plan, and to approve the settlement of the State
budget;

    3. To consider and adopt fundamental policies on finance and monetary matters, the
ratio of the budget deficit, the ratio of Government debt and the ratio of public debt;

    4. To consider and adopt the establishment, amendment, abolition or exemption of
taxes and duties;

    5. To elect or remove the President, the Vice-President and the members of the
Standing Committee of the National Assembly;

    6. To elect or remove the President and the Vice-President of the State;

    7. To elect or remove the Prime Minister;

    8. To elect or remove the President of the People's Supreme Court, the Head of the
Supreme People's Prosecutor's Office, the President of the State Inspection Authority,
the Head of the Supreme Anti-Corruption Authority and the President of the State Audit
Organisation;

    9. To consider and approve the appointment, transfer or removal of the chairmen of
the committees and the heads of the divisions of the National Assembly;

    10. To consider and approve the appointment, transfer or removal of the Deputy Prime
Minister and the members of the Government;

    11. To consider and approve the appointment, transfer or removal of the members of
the panel of judges of the People's Supreme Court;

    12. To consider and adopt the organisational structure of the National Assembly, and
the establishment, relocation or dissolution of the bodies of the National Assembly;

    13. To consider and adopt the organisational structure of the Government, and the
establishment, relocation or dissolution of Ministries, ministry-equivalent
organisations, the capital and the provinces;

    14. To consider and adopt the boundaries of the capital and of the provinces;

    15. To decide on the dissolution of a local people's assembly in the case where that
people's assembly causes serious damage to the interests of the country and the people;

    16. To monitor the respect for and the implementation of the Constitution and the laws
by all State agencies;

    17. To consider and adopt the granting of amnesty;

    18. To consider and adopt the ratification of, participation in, or withdrawal from
treaties and agreements to which the Lao PDR is a party and which affect the fundamental
and important issues of the country;

    19. To consider and adopt matters of war or peace;

    20. To abrogate laws and regulations of any sector that are contrary to the
Constitution and the laws, except for provisions concerning the proceedings of the
People's Court and the People's Prosecutor's Office.

(amended). The Standing Committee of the National Assembly has the following
rights and duties:

    1. To prepare and convene the sessions of the National Assembly;

    2. To propose the elaboration, amendment, interpretation and explanation of the
Constitution and the laws;

    3. To propose draft legislative acts to the President of the State;

    4. To appoint, transfer or remove the heads of the organisational bodies of the
People's Supreme Court and the judges of the people's courts;

    5. To appoint the committee of the Supreme People's Prosecutor's Office;

    6. To appoint the National Election Committee;

    7. To consider and monitor the ratification of, participation in, or withdrawal from
treaties and agreements to which the Lao PDR is a party;

    8. To receive and consider the petitions and proposals of citizens.

(amended). The organisations that have the right to propose legislation are as
follows:

    1. The President of the State;

    2. The Standing Committee of the National Assembly;

    3. The Government;

    4. The People's Supreme Court;

    5. The Supreme People's Prosecutor's Office;

    6. The State Inspection Authority;

    7. The State Audit Organisation;

    8. The Lao Front for National Development, the Lao Veterans' Union and the central
mass organisations.

(amended). The President of the State has the following rights and duties:

    1. To promulgate the Constitution and the laws;

    2. To issue decrees and decree-laws;

    3. To convene and preside over extraordinary meetings of the Government, and to
attend meetings of the Standing Committee of the National Assembly when necessary;

    4. To appoint, transfer or remove the Deputy Prime Minister and the members of the
Government with the approval of the National Assembly;

    5. To appoint, transfer or remove the Deputy President of the People's Supreme Court,
the Deputy Head of the Supreme People's Prosecutor's Office, the Deputy President of the
State Inspection Authority, the Deputy Head of the Supreme Anti-Corruption Authority and
the Deputy President of the State Audit Organisation;

    6. To decide on the promotion, demotion or reduction in rank of officers of the
national defence and public security forces;

    7. To appoint, transfer or remove the heads and deputy heads of the divisions of the
Office of the President of the State;

    8. To appoint plenipotentiary ambassadors of the Lao PDR to foreign countries and to
recall them; to accept plenipotentiary ambassadors or authorised representatives of
foreign countries accredited to the Lao PDR; to decide on the adjustment of positions,
the reduction or the removal of the rank of ambassadors;

    9. To promulgate the appointment of the National Election Committee, the date of the
election, the number of candidates, and the number of members of the National Assembly
and of members of the provincial people's assemblies for each constituency, on the
proposal of the Standing Committee of the National Assembly;

    10. To declare war or peace on the decision of the National Assembly, to declare a
general or partial mobilisation, and to declare a state of emergency throughout the
country or in a locality;

    11. To confer or withdraw State honours, medals, victory medals, the highest
appellations of the State, and to grant other forms of award;

    12. To decide on the remission of sentences and to declare amnesty after the approval
of the National Assembly;

    13. To decide on the granting of asylum to foreigners.

    14. To decide on the conclusion or the signing of treaties; to promulgate the
ratification of, participation in, or withdrawal from treaties in the name of the Lao
PDR.

(amended). The Government has the following rights and duties:

    1. To implement the Constitution, the laws, the resolutions of the sessions of the
National Assembly, the resolutions of the Standing Committee of the National Assembly,
and the decrees and decree-laws of the President of the State;

    2. To submit draft laws to the National Assembly, draft decrees to the Standing
Committee of the National Assembly and draft decree-laws to the President of the State;

    3. To issue regulations and resolutions concerning the management of the economy and
society, science, technology, national resources, the environment, national defence and
public security, and foreign affairs;

    4. To determine the vision, strategies, socio-economic development plans and the State
budget plan, and to submit them to the National Assembly for consideration and approval;

    5. To propose to the National Assembly for consideration and approval the
establishment, relocation or dissolution of ministries, ministry-equivalent
organisations, the capital and the provinces, and the determination of the boundaries of
the capital and of the provinces;

    6. To decide on the establishment, relocation or dissolution of departments and
department-equivalent organisations;

    7. To decide on the establishment, relocation or dissolution of cities and
municipalities and the determination of the boundaries of cities and municipalities,
after approval by the provincial people's assembly;

    8. To establish or dissolve special economic zones, specific economic zones and
international border crossings;

    9. To guide, monitor and inspect the work of the provincial administrations, the local
administrative organisations and the national defence and public security forces;

    10. To decide on the granting, revocation and return of nationality, and on the
restoration of Lao nationality;

    11. To decide on granting honorary Lao citizenship to foreigners;

    12. To decide on the conclusion of treaties; to consider and approve participation in,
or withdrawal from, treaties and agreements in the name of the Government of the Lao PDR;

    13. To suspend or cancel decisions, orders, instructions and recommendations of the
provinces, of organisations under the Government and of local administrative
organisations that are contrary to the law and other regulations, except for decisions
concerning the court proceedings of the people's courts and of the people's prosecutor's
offices;

    14. To report on its work to the National Assembly, the Standing Committee of the
National Assembly and the President of the State.

(amended). The provincial people's assembly has the following rights and
duties:

    1. To consider and approve the general binding regulations of the locality;

    2. To consider and approve the socio-economic development plan, the State budget plan
and the adjustment of the State budget plan of the locality;

    3. To elect or remove the President, the Vice-President and the members of the Standing
Committee of the provincial people's assembly;

    4. To consider and approve the appointment, transfer or removal of the chairman of the
governing board and the head of the office of the provincial people's assembly;

    5. To consider and approve the organisational structure of the provincial people's
assembly, and the establishment, relocation or dissolution of the bodies of the provincial
people's assembly;

    6. To consider and approve the proposal on the appointment, transfer or removal of the
chairman of the governing board of the capital and of the province;

    7. To consider and approve the organisational structure of the governing board of the
capital and of the province; the proposal on the appointment, transfer or removal of the
deputy chairmen of the governing board of the capital and of the province, the heads of
sectors and the heads of ministry-equivalent organisations of the capital and of the
province, and the chairman of the governing board of the city and of the municipality;

    8. To consider and approve the proposal of the governing board of the capital and of
the province concerning the establishment, relocation or dissolution of sectors and
ministry-equivalent organisations of the capital and of the province;

    9. To consider and approve the proposal on the appointment, transfer or removal of the
President of the People's Court of the capital, of the province and of the district;

    10. To consider and approve the proposal on the appointment, transfer or removal of the
head of the People's Prosecutor's Office of the capital, of the province and of the
district;

    11. To consider and approve the establishment or dissolution of cities, municipalities
and towns, and the determination of the boundaries of cities, municipalities and towns;

    12. To monitor the respect for and the implementation of the Constitution and the laws
by State agencies in the locality;

    13. To suspend or cancel decisions, orders, instructions and other regulations of the
sectors at the local level or of the lower level that are contrary to the law, except for
decisions concerning the court proceedings of the people's courts and of the people's
prosecutor's offices.

(amended). The Standing Committee of the provincial people's assembly has the
following rights and duties:

    1. To prepare and convene the sessions of the provincial people's assembly;

    2. To consider and approve the proposal on the appointment, transfer or removal of
judges of the people's courts as determined by law;

    3. To consider and approve the proposal on the appointment of the committee of the
people's prosecutor's office of the capital, of the province and of the district;

    4. To appoint the local election committee;

    5. To receive and consider the petitions and proposals of citizens within the scope of
its responsibility.

(amended). Local administrative organisations have the following rights and
duties:

    1. To organise the implementation of the Constitution, the laws, legislative acts, the
resolutions of the local people's assembly, and the socio-economic development plan and the
budget plan of the locality;

    2. To elaborate the socio-economic development plan, the budget plan and the work plan
for national defence and public security, and to submit them to the local people's assembly
for consideration and approval;

    3. To manage the State in the fields of politics, economy, culture and society, national
defence and public security, natural resources, the environment and other fields;

    4. To issue regulations concerning the management of the economy, culture and society,
and national defence and public security;

    5. To monitor and inspect the performance of duties of the organisations under their
responsibility;

    6. To manage Lao citizens, foreigners, stateless persons and foreign nationals who
reside and move within their locality;

    7. To receive, consider and resolve the proposals of the people;

    8. To liaise and cooperate with foreign countries in accordance with the assignment of
the Government;

    9. To summarise and report on the overall work of implementation in the locality to the
higher level and to the local people's assembly periodically.


                                Part X

              PEOPLE'S COURTS AND PEOPLE'S PROSECUTOR'S OFFICES

(new). The inspection organisations have the following rights and duties:

    1. To inspect the implementation of policies, laws, plans, programmes, projects, the
State budget and State assets, and the rights, duties and responsibilities of organisations;

    2. To inspect the appointment, the performance of duties and responsibilities of
officials, civil servants, soldiers, police, employees of State enterprises and other
persons who commit offences;

    3. To inspect the organisations implementing inspection work and the organisations
implementing the enforcement of court judgments effectively in respect of economic cases
and corruption cases;

    4. To inspect the State investment projects from the outset, to inspect the
implementation of audit work according to the resolution of the National Assembly or of the
local people's assembly, and to verify assets and liabilities;

    5. To consider and resolve complaints; to suppress and combat corruption; to conduct the
investigation and interrogation of corruption cases and of cases connected with corruption;

    6. To propose measures and remedies for the shortcomings of inspection work;

    7. To report on its work, especially the work of inspection and the work of resolving
complaints, to the session of the National Assembly or of the local people's assembly.
